<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>(Left) Hard-violation rate vs. underlying violation probability <math id="S10.F2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. The post-hoc-audit curve scales linearly with <math id="S10.F2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>; the SARC curve is flat at zero because PAG blocks before dispatch. Under the modeled assumptions, the structural distinction is preserved as <math id="S10.F2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> varies. (Right) Escalation queue mean wait <math id="S10.F2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. utilization <math id="S10.F2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, M/M/c. As <math id="S10.F2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\to 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\to 1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S10.F2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> diverges super-linearly. The reversibility window <math id="S10.F2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{\text{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{\text{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math> (dashed) defines the operationally admissible region. The architect’s task is to keep <math id="S10.F2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\lambda_{e},c,\mu)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\lambda_{e},c,\mu)</annotation></semantics></math> in the region where <math id="S10.F2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{q}&lt;\tau_{\text{rev}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mtext>rev</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{q}&lt;\tau_{\text{rev}}</annotation></semantics></math>; once the system leaves that region, escalation is structurally too slow regardless of operator effort.</figcaption>
</figure>
